That's common with 'inertial bounce' expressions, which are meant for overshoot wobble, not a ball under gravity. They jitter if the values don't match your layer.

Here's a simpler one that simulates a real bounce from scratch. Put it on the Position property of the ball:

```javascript
var floor = 900;      // y position of the ground in px
var h = 600;          // drop height in px
var g = 3000;         // gravity in px/s^2, tweak for feel
var e = 0.6;          // bounciness, 0 to 1
var t = time - inPoint;
var v0 = Math.sqrt(2 * g * h);
var tFall = Math.sqrt(2 * h / g);
var y;
if (t < tFall) {
  y = floor - h + 0.5 * g * t * t;
} else {
  t -= tFall;
  var v = v0 * e;
  while (t > 2 * v / g && v > 1) { t -= 2 * v / g; v *= e; }
  y = (v > 1) ? floor - (v * t - 0.5 * g * t * t) : floor;
}
[value[0], y];
```

No keyframes needed. Adjust g until the timing feels right and e for how bouncy it is.
